<p>Seconding Conwell in full. Also:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation placed an order for one unit prior to enacting removal of the listing. 24 hours after placement of the order, a box containing an instance of SCP-4000 was discovered outside Site 133.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like this should at least say &quot;[researcher's name] at Site 133 placed an order&quot; rather than just &quot;the Foundation ordered one&quot;. It's already hard to believe that they'd just put a classified facility as their mailing address instead of a safehouse with a street address or something. Or a front company with a street address. This just seems unrealistic.</p>
<p>I admire the effort put into this, especially as a first SCP, but there are definitely some issues I can't ignore.</p>
<p>I'm still torn on Threat Level. On the one hand, I like the addition of another cool-sounding category with sci-fi flare in SCP articles. On the other hand, I always liked that the Foundation didn't bother with assessing the severity of threat(s) posed by an anomaly because the only thing that matters to them is containment, and containment difficulty is objective while the severity of a threat can be pretty subjective.</p>

							</div>

								
